{"name":"Shamrock Field","hex":"#358d52","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shamrock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The trefoil leaf of any small clover, especially Trifolium repens, or such a leaf from a clover-like plant, commonly used as a symbol of Ireland.","Any of several species of small clover-like plant species, with trefoil leaves, especially Trifolium repens."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shamrock"},{"term":"field","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A land area free of woodland, cities, and towns; an area of open country.","The open country near or belonging to a town or city."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To intercept or catch (a ball) and play it.","To be the team catching and throwing the ball, as opposed to hitting it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/field"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shamrock Field","blocks":[{"text":"Shamrock Field was a Gaelic Athletic Association ground in St. John's, Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada. Originally dating from 1838, it had a capacity of approximately 4,000. During World War II, the sports ground was used as a training camp and billet for members of the Newfoundland Militia. It was also sometimes used as the 'national stadium' of Canada GAA."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamrock_Field"},{"title":"Shamrock","blocks":[{"text":"A shamrock is a type of clover, used as a symbol of Ireland. The word shamrock comes from Irish seamróg, which is the diminutive of the Irish word seamair and simply means \"young clover\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Irish_clover.svg/330px-Irish_clover.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamrock"},{"title":"Spring green","blocks":[{"text":"Spring green is a color that was traditionally considered to be on the yellow side of green, but in modern computer systems based on the RGB color model is halfway between cyan and green on the color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Clover_and_woodsorrel.jpg/500px-Clover_and_woodsorrel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Clover_and_woodsorrel.jpg/1280px-Clover_and_woodsorrel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Clover_and_woodsorrel.jpg/500px-Clover_and_woodsorrel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"White clover (Trifolium repens) and yellow wood sorrel (Oxalis stricta)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AClover_and_woodsorrel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Trifolium_dubium_kz1.jpg/500px-Trifolium_dubium_kz1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Trifolium_dubium_kz1.jpg/500px-Trifolium_dubium_kz1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Trifolium dubium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrifolium_dubium_kz1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Shamrock_plant_in_bloom.jpg/500px-Shamrock_plant_in_bloom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Shamrock_plant_in_bloom.jpg/1280px-Shamrock_plant_in_bloom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https:/